20: E1:69  ### 2. The two-minute check that turns "predicted 77/77" into "equivalent by construction".
21: E1:70  
22: E1:71  `src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c` at HEAD: `tileptr = d->re_tlb_[abc][tile_nr] << 16` (`:258-262`), `tileptr = 0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[tile_nr] << 12)` (`:267`), the valid-bit test `if (!(tileptr & 0x80000000))` (`:312`), then **`tileptr &= ~0x80000000;` at `:319`** before the stores at `:374`/`:377`.  Every descriptor the committed probe drives is `0x8001..0x8005` (`:447-449`, `:704`) and the linear tile is `0x10000` (`:95`), so at the guarded store `tileptr` is in `{0x10000, 0x20000, 0x30000, 0x40000, 0x50000}` and `tileptr & 0xfff00000 == 0` on EVERY row.  `if (ok || (tileptr & 0xfff00000))` is therefore textually `if (ok)` on every driven row: 89/89 at HEAD by construction, not by prediction.  The same inspection also classifies it under D1: bit 31 IS a body-tested mask (`:312`), so it belongs to the generating set -- and it is constant-cleared before the store, so the `0xfff00000` slice touches no tested bit at the point of the guard.  Bits 20-30 are untested.  Clause (e), by the letter.
23: E1:72  
24: E1:73  ### 3. Ruling, with its warrant and bound.
25: E1:74  
26: E1:75  **"Closable by a finite row set" is not a discriminator, and the carrier already rejected it.**  On 2026-09-04 the main loop's *"pairs of body-read lvalues over a finite reachable space"* was rejected as SELF-REFUTING: *"the counts' raw value space is 2^32 apiece, so 'finite' does no bounding work ... (a C int is finite)"*.  The measuring seat's floor warrant -- *"pure functions of (x, y, bufdepth, tlb_mode), so the free input set is finite"* -- is that bound back under a new name: `(x, y)` ranges over 2048 x 128 after `y &= 127`, and C1-C4 SAMPLE it (the probe's own vocabulary, `:595`).  And its debt warrant is the same error inverted: *"a slice within an already-driven class is not closable by any finite row set"* (`:610-611`) is true of the FAMILY of slices and false of every member -- `x & 1` dies to one odd-`x` row, `y & 2` to one row with `y` bit 1 set, `mode & 1` to one mode word with bit 0 set, `tileptr & 0xfff00000` to one descriptor `>= 0x8010`.  Finiteness is identical in both buckets: each single predicate is one-row closable; the family of predicates is unbounded.  So it separates nothing, which is why the round graded one species two ways by it.
27: E1:76  
28: E1:77  **The governing discriminator is the one the carrier already carries, read with its bounds.**  (i) Clause (d)'s operand-swap enumeration draws swaps from *fields the guarded BODY READS*, walked up only to the enclosing-function boundary: here `tileptr`, `ofs`, `buf`, `bufdepth`.  Those swaps were all killed at 59 rows (`ok || ofs` 58/59 by `ycrcb x4y1`; `ok || tileptr` by every decline row, since `tileptr` is nonzero everywhere).  (ii) The CELLS owed are the cross-product of the guard's dominating predicates with the body-TESTED partitions of the device-state lvalues the repair path reads (pass 11, 09-04), where "tests" means masks or compares as a case (D1, 09-25).  (iii) A value, bit or slice the body never tests as a case is clause (e), disclosed; consequence orders the debt queue and never promotes to floor (D1: `#549`'s latch x bit was graded (e) although its consequence was the round's own defect).
29: E1:78  
30: E1:79  Applied to `#519`'s survivors, this gives ONE answer for all of them:
